# Key Learning
This room demonstrated a complete four-stage privilege escalation chain where no single vulnerability required root access. NoSQL injection bypassed authentication by exploiting operator injection in a JSON-accepting login endpoint. EJS template injection turned a seemingly benign preview feature into arbitrary code execution. A Node.js debug port left open in production allowed lateral movement to a second service account via the Chrome DevTools Protocol. Finally, unnecessary disk group membership converted a low-privileged service account into full raw filesystem access using debugfs, bypassing all Linux permission enforcement without a password or kernel exploit. Each stage exploited a configuration mistake rather than a zero-day, reinforcing that defence-in-depth at the configuration level is as important as patching.

# What I Would Flag in a Real SOC
- JSON login endpoints that accept MongoDB or NeDB operator objects without input sanitisation are vulnerable to NoSQL injection authentication bypass, all query inputs must be validated as strings before being passed to database query constructors
- EJS or any other template engine called with ejs.render() on unsanitised user input is a critical SSTI vulnerability, template rendering must only accept trusted static templates with user input passed as data variables, never as the template string itself
- Node.js processes running with --inspect or --inspect-brk flags in production expose the Chrome DevTools Protocol, which allows unauthenticated arbitrary code execution for any user with local network access to the debug port, debug ports must never be enabled in production and must be bound to localhost only during development
- A service account belonging to the disk group has effective read and write access to all data on every raw block device on the system, this is functionally equivalent to root access for data confidentiality and integrity purposes and must never be granted to application service accounts
- The ability to read /root/root.txt via debugfs without any root credentials confirms that filesystem-level access controls provided zero protection once the disk group membership was obtained, group membership audits must include disk, shadow, sudo, and docker as equivalent to root escalation paths
- Lateral movement from poolside to pipelinesvc via a local debug port would appear in network logs only as loopback traffic and would generate no authentication events, detection requires process-level monitoring of WebSocket connections to localhost:9229 and alerting on Runtime.evaluate calls from unexpected source processes
